e  total cost should be one hundred  thousand why doesn't it leave off this  last line item my guess is Ned never  even considered this costly tourney a  real possibility him going through all  these details would just be a waste of  time this is yet another example of  Ned's naivete coming in as hand of the  king leave this episode notice the  Dothraki word that Dany is trying to  pronounce at chucka Oh chucka Oh chucka  in Dothraki  at jakar translates to pride the term is  derived from the Daath word to hawk the  name of the long braid worn by Dothraki  warriors that they only cut if they ever  lose and  it's a physical symbol of their pride  and look closely at what Daenerys is  doing here she's having her hair braided  forming her own jihad and this happens  right after she won her first victory  deflating the pride of her cruel brother  Viserys one of my favorite miscible  details from the whole series is that  Danny's jayhawk season after season  becomes longer and more complex and it  all starts in this moment okay let's  move on to the deeper meaning of this  episode Daenerys is successfully  adapting to her surroundings but she's  not the only one to go through that kind  of transition this episode the title  Lord snow is actually taken from a  character we haven't really talked about  in this video yet Jon Snow  well it's know he is you're the least  useless person the Lord snow is of  course thorns Maquis nickname for John a  proud new comer who's too frosty to make  allies he talks down to and alienates  all of his fellow Knights watch recruits  immediately but as I said before this  episode is comparing John to another  Lord snow Ned Stark and his first few  minutes of arriving in the capital Ned  immediately dismisses various he mocks  Littlefinger to losing to his brother  Brandon and the duel and he implies that  pycelle is disloyal come on Ned that's  way too frosty in the books Littlefinger  actually calls out Ned's icy demeanor  joking that Starks are made of ice and  melt below the neck and Ned coolly  responds that he doesn't plan on melting  anytime soon and then in this episode  Littlefinger sums up the Starks with  this line  and I like how the editing immediately  jumps to the other Lord snow after this  line even though Jon and Ned are both  frosty men bitter over being misled into  coming to this rotten dead-end John  after heating the wisdom of Benjamin  Tyrion learns to adapt to his new  surroundings by the end of the episode  he helps his brothers rather than  talking down to them and beating the  crap out of him making more enemies than  allies will always be a struggle for Jon  throughout the series but he at least  makes the right friends for Ned on the  other hand stubbornness is his tragic  flaw and his honorable refusal to play  this game Thrones will lead him to die  as Lord snow a question for you guys do  you think this scenes not in the book  and proved the writing on the show or do  you think the show really needs George  RR Martin's dialogue comments down below  and tweet me your thoughts directly at  EA boss or follow new rockstars on  Twitter at new rockstars for updates on  our videos now if you like thinking  deeply about your favorite shows like I  do and impressing your friends with how  smart you are another channel besides  one that I can't recommend enough is  wisecrack specifically their quick take  series is great  Jared's video on the philosophy of  Westworld in a way it uses music to tell  a story is exactly the kind of  thoughtful video essay we aim to do here  definitely give it a watch and subscribe  to wisecrack by clicking the link in the  description below and yeah I subscribe  to us here at new rock stars for more  Game of Thrones analysis I'm gonna try  to keep making these season when we  watch videos no promises on release  schedule because we cover a lot here but  you can help by liking and sharing this  video around and by contributing to us  on patreon big th